  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1000 PM MDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nMain moisture plume now mostly east of the CWA as the upper trough \nmoves out over the central Plains. The tail does extend back across \nHudspeth County, where enough instability persists for a few \nthunderstorms to move through Hudspeth and far east El Paso \nCounties over the next 3-5 hours. Radar is showing fairly strong \nstorms originating near Samalayuca and now into far SE El Paso \nCounty with high reflective and MESH readings. Will have to watch \nfor possible severe next several hours. Elsewhere a few stray \nstorms have formed over the southern Sacs but are rapidly moving \neast out of the area. \n\nSaturday and Sunday...ridging from the sub-tropical high develops \nover the area behind the exiting trough, bringing in mostly dry \nweather with suppressed cloud development. Both GFS/ECMWF show a \nvort center or possible MCS over the Sierra Madres of Mexico \nSaturday and this feature moves some moisture up late Sunday night. \nGFS actually brings some rain up late Sunday night to areas west of \nthe RG Valley. \n\nMonday and Tuesday...we await the arrival of Polo remnants Monday \nand Tuesday as it gradually gets absorbed by mid-latitude low \nsweeping into Arizona Tuesday. Latest GFS run brings the remnants \nacross west Texas, while the ECMWF brings it a bit further west over \nsouth central New Mexico. GFS corresponds better to official NHC \nforecast track. Both scenarios would have some potential dangerous \nimpacts on our area, but the ECMWF path would especially impact \nthe entire CWA. PWs increase to around 1.4 inches with the \naforementioned vort center and then as high as 1.5 to 1.6 inches \nMonday night and Tuesday. These kinds of PWs are around 220% of \nnormal and likely record highs. Obviously we are looking at \nanother round of high flood potential, though briefer than this \nrecent event we just experienced. GFS also keeps well defined \nsurface reflection across west Texas Tuesday afternoon for some \ngusty northwest winds. \n\nWednesday into the weekend...both GFS/ECMWF show the mid-latitude \nlow over central or western New Mexico Wednesday afternoon.\nTropical moisture is east of the area Wednesday as PWs drop to under \none inch. Upper low will still provide lift and with modest \ninstability, isolated thunderstorms are still possible. By Thursday \nthe upper low is out over the central Plains (slightly slower in the \nECMWF), with short wave ridge moving over the area.\nNext Pacific long wave trough moves over the Great Basin. After \nWednesday we should see nice drying out with plenty of sunshine and \nseasonable temperatures.",
